" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362207 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186361109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362207 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fd49cbaa-d57f-5e0e-9cec-db71bf257efe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362318 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470592 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362318 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470593 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362318 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470594 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362318 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98714" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362318 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362318 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6b/6b9b15bd3c8cda84156d5461fb9f80f892a1dba26151cc37d381757f9f659fa1.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y=-3$ is a node.\n\nII. $y=2$ is a sink.\n\nIII. $y=2$ is a source.\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362318 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e1d8d74b-55cf-51de-803f-0cecc60a4fb1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362318 #edb/ref 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/36/362ffe8871efa8dc597708b039fa766c3603777ba12460e84d9af1fc39800288.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-3$ is a node.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=2$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362323 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362323 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98757" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362323 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362323 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=2y-{y}^{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362323 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bd711689-2039-5e3a-97e9-854277e0ac5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362323 #edb/ref 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ 2y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(2-y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2-y$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ),$ and positive for $y\\in (0,2).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](images/df/df68345e3424f1e0020a98ef8dd13456865412818067a822c01c3f50e49386ec.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=0$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\n- $y=2$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=0$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a sink\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374986 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374986 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340344" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374986 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374986 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=(y-3)({y}^{2}+5)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374986 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b343081e-f618-50fb-8cd3-8bac934f3e0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374986 #edb/ref 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ (y-3)({y}^{2}+5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solution is $y=3.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y-3$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| ${y}^{2}+5$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (3,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,3).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](images/bb/bb9204b3eb9bcf7fe536508f5635356aacdbd8e29f276b03bac9dddd916609bb.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solution:\n\n- $y=3$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=3$ is a source\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374987 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374987 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374987 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374987 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374987 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374987 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374987 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374987 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186374992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374987 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d6858d42-6ccf-559c-a286-454ea7a3acd2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374988 #edb/ref 1080 "$y=3$ is a node" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374988 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374988 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "01b51afd-17cc-5f52-afd7-77de192930a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374989 #edb/ref 1080 "$y=3$ is a source and $y=-3$ is a sink" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374989 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374989 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "61b64586-d698-5841-adf5-803349002047" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374990 #edb/ref 1080 "$y=3$ is a sink" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374990 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374990 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b9a800f7-3027-5549-993a-ce836a2294ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374991 #edb/ref 1080 "$y=3$ is a sink and $y=-3$ is a source" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374991 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374991 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e135b204-c1bc-5ec5-950b-2e613adeb18b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374992 #edb/ref 1080 "$y=3$ is a source" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374992 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374992 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2d98202e-2b70-5bc6-b94a-b9e318f37f2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395542 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395542 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98781" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395542 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395542 #edb/ref 1071 "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-3$ is a sink\n\n- $y=0$ is a node\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395542 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "55d1795f-d6ac-5ff7-a9de-20bb6292950d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395542 #edb/ref 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=-3$ and $y=0.$\n\n- We're given that $y=-3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=-3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=0$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=0$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just below $y=0$ points downward, the arrow just above it points downward too.\n\n![](images/db/db7e9ea076c3a1fe2cf5341b841693e36d5b9a9030e624da88d2cfd3b0d3a1b0.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\